g is assumed to be a linear discriminant function over the joint features of the input and output f(x,y); W denotes a weight vector and 〈,〉 denotes a dot product between two vectors. The trained function g parametrized by W should be constraint to assign a larger score to the actual output y
i than all other possible wrong ys for each input x
i in the training data. Hence, the learning is formulated as minimizing the violations from this constraint. A more sophisticated technique is to consider the loss between a wrong y and the ground truth y
i during the minimization (see Section Loss function). However, in the structured output problems, usually there is a large number of possible ys per x
i that leads to a large number of learning constraints when searching for the optimum W based on the above idea. A solution to this problem is to consider only the most violated y for each x. In other words, at each training iteration the following inference is solved per training example,
